Make a click in the icon manager behave like a taskbar.

f.iconify is an unconditional toggle, which is wrong for the icon manager when
it is used as a taskbar: every click iconified the window, so clicking the entry
of a window that was on screen made it vanish, and there was no way to switch
between two windows without minimising one on the way.

There is no way to express this in a .ctwmrc -- ctwm has no conditional
function, and none of f.iconify / f.deiconify / f.raise / f.focus alone covers
the three cases -- so it has to happen here.

Scoped to C_ICONMGR so the title bar's minimise button, and f.iconify from a
menu or a key binding, keep the unconditional toggle they have always had.

--- a/functions_win.c
+++ b/functions_win.c
@@ -182,6 +182,46 @@
 static void
 iconify_handler(EF_FULLPROTO)
 {
+#ifdef __LIKEOS__
+	/*
+	 * Taskbar behaviour, for clicks in the icon manager only.
+	 *
+	 * A click on an entry ACTIVATES that window; only a click on the entry
+	 * of the window that already holds the focus minimises it.  Plain
+	 * f.iconify toggles unconditionally, so from a taskbar every click
+	 * iconified: clicking a visible window's entry made it disappear, and
+	 * switching between two windows was impossible without minimising one on
+	 * the way there.
+	 *
+	 * The three states, in the order a user meets them:
+	 *   minimised            -> restore it and focus it
+	 *   visible, not focused -> raise it and focus it  (plain switch)
+	 *   visible and focused  -> minimise it            (the toggle)
+	 *
+	 * Only C_ICONMGR takes this path, so the title bar's minimise button and
+	 * f.iconify from a menu or key binding keep the unconditional toggle.
+	 */
+	if(context == C_ICONMGR && func == F_ICONIFY) {
+		if(tmp_win->isicon) {
+			DeIconify(tmp_win);
+		}
+		else if(Scr->Focus != tmp_win) {
+			OtpRaise(tmp_win, WinWin);
+			WMapRaise(tmp_win);
+		}
+		else {
+			Iconify(tmp_win, eventp->xbutton.x_root - 5,
+			        eventp->xbutton.y_root - 5);
+			return;
+		}
+		/* Windows that decline the input focus are left alone, exactly as
+		 * the pointer-driven focus path does. */
+		if(tmp_win->wmhints && tmp_win->wmhints->input) {
+			SetFocus(tmp_win, CurrentTime);
+		}
+		return;
+	}
+#endif
 	if(tmp_win->isicon) {
 		DeIconify(tmp_win);
 	}
